          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following is a distinguishing feature of Anopheles mosquitoes compa</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following is a distinguishing feature of Anopheles mosquitoes compared to Aedes and Culex?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Larvae hang head-down from the water surface using a siphon</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Head-down larval posture with a siphon is characteristic of Aedes and Culex, not Anopheles. Anopheles larvae lack a prominent siphon and lie parallel to the water surface.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adults rest at a 45° angle to the surface</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Anopheles adults rest at a characteristic 45° angle, unlike Aedes and Culex which rest with the body parallel to the surface. This is one of the most reliable field-identification features.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Breeds preferentially in domestic containers with clean water</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Clean domestic water containers (coolers, tyres, flower pots) are the breeding sites of Aedes aegypti. Anopheles breeds in clean, slow-moving or stagnant natural water bodies.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bites predominantly during daytime hours</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Anopheles is a night-biter (dusk to dawn). Aedes is the day-biting genus — this biting-time difference directly influences when personal protection measures must be used.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the comparison table: Anopheles rests at 45°, larva lies parallel to the surface; Aedes and Culex rest parallel, larvae hang head-down via a siphon."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! The 45° resting angle and parallel larval position are the two most high-yield identification features of Anopheles, the primary malaria vector."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
