          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the mechanism by which DDT (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane) kills insec</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the mechanism by which DDT (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane) kills insects?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inhibits acetylcholinesterase at synaptic junctions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is the mechanism of organophosphates (e.g., malathion) and carbamates (e.g., propoxur), not organochlorines like DDT.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Keeps voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, causing repetitive neuronal firing and paralysis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. DDT prevents normal closing of voltage-gated sodium channels, leading to uncontrolled repetitive nerve impulses, paralysis, and death — the classic organochlorine mechanism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Blocks GABA-gated chloride channels, causing neuronal excitotoxicity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Although some insecticides (e.g., fipronil, cyclodienes) act on GABA receptors, DDT specifically acts on voltage-gated sodium channels in the insect nervous system.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inhibits chitin synthesis, preventing larval moulting</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Chitin synthesis inhibitors (e.g., diflubenzuron) are insect growth regulators that disrupt the exoskeleton. DDT is a neurotoxin that acts on sodium channels.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Each insecticide class has a distinct mechanism: DDT (Na⁺ channels), organophosphates and carbamates (acetylcholinesterase), and growth regulators (chitin synthesis). Review the comparison."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent! DDT's sodium-channel mechanism explains both its high efficacy and the cross-resistance seen with pyrethroids, which share a similar target site."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
